Language.Syntax Basics:
# Syntax Basics

This page covers the shape of a Dart file: comments, statements, entry points, and printing output.

## Every program starts at `main`

```dart
void main() {
  print('Hello, Dart!');
}
```

- `main` is the entry point Dart looks for when you run a file directly.
- `void` means the function returns nothing.
- Statements end with a semicolon `;`.
- Blocks are wrapped in curly braces `{ }`.

## Comments

```dart
// Single-line comment

/*
  Multi-line
  comment
*/

/// Documentation comment (used by `dart doc` to generate API docs)
/// Supports Markdown.
void greet() {}
```

## `print` and string interpolation

```dart
String name = 'psdk';
int age = 30;

print('Hello, $name!');            // simple variable interpolation
print('Next year you will be ${age + 1}'); // expression interpolation with ${}
```

Any expression can go inside `${}`; a bare `$identifier` is a shorthand for simple variable access.

## Semicolons and whitespace

Dart is not whitespace-sensitive (unlike Python). Indentation is a style convention, enforced automatically by `dart format`, not a language rule. Every statement needs a trailing semicolon:

```dart
int x = 1;
int y = 2;
print(x + y);
```

## Case sensitivity and identifiers

- Dart is case-sensitive: `myVar` and `MyVar` are different identifiers.
- Convention: `lowerCamelCase` for variables and functions, `UpperCamelCase` for classes/types, `lowercase_with_underscores` for file names and package names.
- Identifiers cannot start with a digit, and cannot be a reserved keyword (`class`, `if`, `for`, `void`, etc).

## `main` with command-line arguments

```dart
void main(List<String> arguments) {
  if (arguments.isEmpty) {
    print('No arguments given.');
  } else {
    print('Arguments: $arguments');
  }
}
```

Next: [Variables and Types](/dart-lang/3.13.3/language/variables-and-types/).

Language.Control Flow:
# Control Flow

## `if` / `else`

```dart
int score = 85;

if (score >= 90) {
  print('A');
} else if (score >= 80) {
  print('B');
} else {
  print('C or below');
}
```

## Ternary and null-aware operators

```dart
String result = score >= 60 ? 'pass' : 'fail';

String? nickname;
String display = nickname ?? 'Anonymous';       // use 'Anonymous' if nickname is null
nickname ??= 'psdk';                              // assign only if currently null

int? length = nickname?.length;                   // null-aware access; null if nickname is null
```

## `switch` statements and expressions

Classic statement form:

```dart
switch (score ~/ 10) {
  case 10:
  case 9:
    print('A');
    break;
  case 8:
    print('B');
    break;
  default:
    print('C or below');
}
```

Modern switch **expression** (Dart 3+), often more concise:

```dart
String grade = switch (score ~/ 10) {
  10 || 9 => 'A',
  8 => 'B',
  7 => 'C',
  _ => 'F',
};
```

`_` is the wildcard/default pattern, `||` combines multiple matching values.

## Loops

```dart
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  print(i);
}

for (final item in ['a', 'b', 'c']) {
  print(item);
}

int i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
  print(i);
  i++;
}

int j = 0;
do {
  print(j);
  j++;
} while (j < 5);
```

## `break` and `continue`

```dart
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  if (i == 3) continue; // skip this iteration
  if (i == 6) break;    // exit the loop entirely
  print(i);
}
```

## Labeled loops

For breaking out of nested loops:

```dart
outer:
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
    if (j == 1) continue outer;
    print('$i,$j');
  }
}
```

## Pattern matching with `switch` (destructuring)

Dart 3 patterns let you match on structure, not just values:

```dart
var point = (3, 4); // a record, see Collections page

switch (point) {
  case (0, 0):
    print('origin');
  case (var x, var y) when x == y:
    print('on the diagonal');
  case (var x, var y):
    print('at ($x, $y)');
}
```

Next: [Collections](/dart-lang/3.13.3/language/collections/).

Language.Collections:
# Collections

## Lists

```dart
List<int> numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

numbers.add(6);
numbers.remove(1);        // removes the value 1, not index 1
numbers.removeAt(0);      // removes by index
print(numbers.first);
print(numbers.last);
print(numbers.length);
print(numbers.contains(3));

var doubled = numbers.map((n) => n * 2).toList();
var evens = numbers.where((n) => n.isEven).toList();
var sum = numbers.fold<int>(0, (acc, n) => acc + n);
numbers.sort();
numbers.sort((a, b) => b.compareTo(a)); // descending

final fixed = List<int>.unmodifiable([1, 2, 3]); // read-only list
```

Spread and conditional elements:

```dart
List<int> a = [1, 2, 3];
List<int> b = [0, ...a, 4];       // spread: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]

bool includeExtra = true;
List<int> c = [
  1,
  2,
  if (includeExtra) 3,             // conditional element
  for (var n in a) n * 10,         // list comprehension-style loop
];
```

## Maps

```dart
Map<String, int> ages = {
  'psdk': 30,
  'alex': 25,
};

ages['sam'] = 40;             // add/update
ages.remove('alex');
print(ages['psdk']);          // 30
print(ages.containsKey('sam'));

for (final entry in ages.entries) {
  print('${entry.key}: ${entry.value}');
}

ages.forEach((key, value) => print('$key -> $value'));
```

## Sets

```dart
Set<String> tags = {'dart', 'linux', 'cli'};
tags.add('dart');       // no-op, already present
tags.remove('cli');
print(tags.contains('dart'));

var a = {1, 2, 3};
var b = {2, 3, 4};
print(a.union(b));         // {1, 2, 3, 4}
print(a.intersection(b));  // {2, 3}
print(a.difference(b));    // {1}
```

## Records (Dart 3+)

Lightweight, anonymous, immutable groups of values — no class needed:

```dart
(String, int) person = ('psdk', 30);
print(person.$1); // 'psdk'
print(person.$2); // 30

// Named fields
({String name, int age}) namedPerson = (name: 'psdk', age: 30);
print(namedPerson.name);

(String, int) getUser() => ('psdk', 30);

var (name, age) = getUser(); // destructuring
print('$name is $age');
```

Records are great for returning multiple values from a function without declaring a whole class.

## Iterables

`List`, `Set`, and the results of `map`/`where` are all `Iterable`. Common operations:

```dart
Iterable<int> nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

print(nums.any((n) => n > 4));     // true
print(nums.every((n) => n > 0));   // true
print(nums.reduce((a, b) => a + b)); // 15
print(nums.take(2));               // (1, 2)
print(nums.skip(2));               // (3, 4, 5)
print(nums.toList());
```

Next: [Functions](/dart-lang/3.13.3/language/functions/).

Language.Functions:
# Functions

## Basic syntax

```dart
int add(int a, int b) {
  return a + b;
}

// Arrow syntax for single-expression bodies
int addArrow(int a, int b) => a + b;

void main() {
  print(add(2, 3));
  print(addArrow(2, 3));
}
```

## Optional positional parameters

```dart
String greet(String name, [String? title]) {
  if (title != null) return 'Hello, $title $name';
  return 'Hello, $name';
}

greet('psdk');            // Hello, psdk
greet('psdk', 'Dr.');     // Hello, Dr. psdk
```

## Named parameters

```dart
void createUser({required String name, int age = 18, String? email}) {
  print('$name, $age, ${email ?? "no email"}');
}

createUser(name: 'psdk');
createUser(name: 'psdk', age: 30, email: 'psdk@example.com');
```

`required` makes a named parameter mandatory. Named parameters without `required` should generally have a default value or be nullable.

## Functions as values

Functions are first-class values in Dart — they can be stored in variables, passed as arguments, and returned from other functions.

```dart
void Function(String) logger = (msg) => print('[LOG] $msg');
logger('started');

int applyTwice(int Function(int) f, int value) => f(f(value));
print(applyTwice((x) => x * 2, 3)); // 12

// Closures capture surrounding variables
Function makeCounter() {
  int count = 0;
  return () {
    count++;
    return count;
  };
}

var counter = makeCounter();
print(counter()); // 1
print(counter()); // 2
```

## Anonymous functions and arrow bodies

```dart
var numbers = [1, 2, 3];
numbers.forEach((n) {
  print(n * 2);
});

numbers.forEach((n) => print(n * 2)); // shorter, arrow form
```

## Typedefs

Give a function type a name for readability:

```dart
typedef IntTransformer = int Function(int);

int increment(int x) => x + 1;

void apply(IntTransformer f, int value) {
  print(f(value));
}

apply(increment, 5); // 6
```

## Generic functions

```dart
T first<T>(List<T> items) => items[0];

print(first<int>([1, 2, 3]));       // 1
print(first<String>(['a', 'b']));   // a
print(first([1, 2, 3]));            // type inferred, no need to specify <int>
```

Next: [Classes and OOP](/dart-lang/3.13.3/language/classes-and-oop/).

Language.Classes and OOP:
# Classes & Object-Oriented Programming

## Defining a class

```dart
class Person {
  String name;
  int age;

  Person(this.name, this.age); // constructor with shorthand field assignment

  void introduce() {
    print("Hi, I'm $name and I'm $age years old.");
  }
}

void main() {
  var p = Person('psdk', 30);
  p.introduce();
}
```

## Named constructors

```dart
class Point {
  double x, y;

  Point(this.x, this.y);

  Point.origin() : x = 0, y = 0;

  factory Point.fromMap(Map<String, double> map) {
    return Point(map['x']!, map['y']!);
  }
}

var a = Point(3, 4);
var b = Point.origin();
var c = Point.fromMap({'x': 1, 'y': 2});
```

A `factory` constructor can return an existing instance instead of always creating a new one — useful for caching or returning a subtype.

## Getters and setters

```dart
class Circle {
  double radius;
  Circle(this.radius);

  double get area => 3.14159 * radius * radius;

  set diameter(double d) => radius = d / 2;
}

var c = Circle(5);
print(c.area);
c.diameter = 20; // radius becomes 10
```

## Inheritance

```dart
class Animal {
  String name;
  Animal(this.name);

  void speak() => print('$name makes a sound.');
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  Dog(super.name);

  @override
  void speak() => print('$name barks.');
}

var d = Dog('Rex');
d.speak(); // Rex barks.
```

Call the parent implementation with `super`:

```dart
class LoudDog extends Dog {
  LoudDog(super.name);

  @override
  void speak() {
    super.speak();
    print('...loudly!');
  }
}
```

## Abstract classes and interfaces

Any Dart class can be used as an interface — there's no separate `interface` keyword:

```dart
abstract class Shape {
  double area(); // no body: subclasses must implement it
}

class Square implements Shape {
  double side;
  Square(this.side);

  @override
  double area() => side * side;
}
```

- `extends` — single inheritance, inherits implementation.
- `implements` — adopt the shape (method signatures) of one or more classes/interfaces, providing your own implementation for everything.
- A class can `implements` multiple types but `extends` only one.

## Mixins

Share behavior across unrelated classes without inheritance:

```dart
mixin Swimmer {
  void swim() => print('swimming');
}

mixin Flyer {
  void fly() => print('flying');
}

class Duck with Swimmer, Flyer {}

var duck = Duck();
duck.swim();
duck.fly();
```

## Enums

```dart
enum Status { pending, active, done }

var s = Status.active;
print(s);           // Status.active
print(s.name);       // active
print(Status.values); // [Status.pending, Status.active, Status.done]
```

Enhanced enums (Dart 2.17+) can have fields and methods:

```dart
enum Planet {
  mercury(radiusKm: 2439.7),
  earth(radiusKm: 6371.0);

  final double radiusKm;
  const Planet({required this.radiusKm});
}

print(Planet.earth.radiusKm); // 6371.0
```

## Static members

```dart
class MathUtils {
  static const double pi = 3.14159;
  static double square(double x) => x * x;
}

print(MathUtils.pi);
print(MathUtils.square(4));
```

## Operator overloading

```dart
class Vector {
  final double x, y;
  Vector(this.x, this.y);

  Vector operator +(Vector other) => Vector(x + other.x, y + other.y);

  @override
  String toString() => 'Vector($x, $y)';
}

print(Vector(1, 2) + Vector(3, 4)); // Vector(4.0, 6.0)
```

## Const constructors

A `const` constructor lets you create **compile-time constant objects** — not just constant variables (see [Variables and Types](/dart-lang/3.13.3/language/variables-and-types/)), but entire objects that are built once, at compile time, and can be structurally identical instances that share memory:

```dart
class Point {
  final double x, y;
  const Point(this.x, this.y); // const constructor: every field must be final
}

void main() {
  const p1 = Point(1, 2);
  const p2 = Point(1, 2);

  print(identical(p1, p2)); // true — same compile-time constant, same object

  var p3 = Point(1, 2); // still valid without const, just a normal runtime object
  print(identical(p1, p3)); // false
}
```

Requirements for a `const` constructor:
- Every field the constructor sets must be `final`.
- The constructor body must be empty (`const Point(this.x, this.y);`, not `{ }` with statements inside).
- Any default parameter values must themselves be compile-time constants.

```dart
class Config {
  final String environment;
  final bool debug;

  const Config({this.environment = 'production', this.debug = false});
}

void main() {
  const defaultConfig = Config();
  const devConfig = Config(environment: 'development', debug: true);
}
```

## Why use `const` objects

- **Performance** — identical `const` instances are created once and reused, rather than allocated repeatedly.
- **Correctness** — a `const` object is deeply immutable; it can never change after creation, which makes it safe to share freely without defensive copying.

This matters most for things like configuration objects, enum-like value classes, or (in a Flutter context, though out of scope here) UI widgets — but the underlying mechanism is a plain Dart language feature, not tied to any framework.

## `const` vs `final` on a field

```dart
class Circle {
  static const double pi = 3.14159; // shared compile-time constant across all instances
  final double radius;              // set once per instance, at construction

  const Circle(this.radius);
}
```

`static const` belongs to the class itself and is computed once, ever. A plain `final` instance field is set per-object, when that object is constructed — it can differ between instances even though it can't change after that.

