Language.Null Safety:
# Null Safety

Dart has **sound null safety**: a variable of a non-nullable type can never be `null`, guaranteed at compile time. This eliminates the classic "null pointer exception" as a runtime surprise — the compiler catches it before you ever run the code.

## Nullable vs non-nullable types

```dart
String name = 'psdk';   // non-nullable: can never be null
String? nickname;        // nullable: defaults to null, marked with ?

name = null;   // COMPILE ERROR
nickname = null; // fine
```

Every type is non-nullable by default. Add `?` to allow `null`.

## Null-aware operators

```dart
String? nickname;

// ?? — provide a fallback if null
String display = nickname ?? 'Anonymous';

// ??= — assign only if currently null
nickname ??= 'psdk';

// ?. — safely call a method/access a property, short-circuits to null if the receiver is null
int? len = nickname?.length;

// ?[] — null-aware index access
List<int>? list;
var first = list?[0]; // null, doesn't throw

// ... spread — null-aware spread into a collection
List<int>? maybeList;
var combined = [1, 2, ...?maybeList]; // skips it entirely if null
```

## The `!` (null assertion) operator

```dart
String? maybeName = getName();
String definiteName = maybeName!; // "trust me, this isn't null"
```

If `maybeName` actually is `null` at runtime, this **throws** a runtime error. Use it only when you're certain, from context, that the value can't be null there — overusing `!` defeats the purpose of null safety.

## Smart casting (promotion)

```dart
String? maybeName;

if (maybeName != null) {
  print(maybeName.length); // no ! needed — Dart knows it's non-null here
}
```

This only works for local variables and final fields the analyzer can prove aren't reassigned between the check and the use.

## Late variables and null safety

```dart
late String description; // "I promise to set this before reading it"

class Service {
  late final String config; // set once, in initState/constructor body, not at declaration
}
```

Reading a `late` variable before it's assigned throws a `LateInitializationError` — a clear runtime signal rather than a silent `null`.

## Required named parameters

Null safety pairs naturally with `required`:

```dart
void createUser({required String name, String? email}) { ... }
```

`name` must always be passed; `email` is optional and nullable.

