Language.Generics and Advanced Types:
# Generics & Advanced Types

## Generic classes

```dart
class Box<T> {
  T value;
  Box(this.value);

  void printType() => print(T);
}

var intBox = Box<int>(42);
var stringBox = Box<String>('hello');
intBox.printType(); // int
```

## Bounded type parameters

Restrict a generic type to subtypes of something:

```dart
class NumberBox<T extends num> {
  T value;
  NumberBox(this.value);

  T doubled() => (value * 2) as T;
}

var b = NumberBox<int>(5);
print(b.doubled()); // 10

// NumberBox<String>('x'); // COMPILE ERROR — String doesn't extend num
```

## Generic methods

```dart
T firstWhereOrDefault<T>(List<T> list, bool Function(T) test, T fallback) {
  for (final item in list) {
    if (test(item)) return item;
  }
  return fallback;
}
```

## `extension` methods

Add functionality to existing types — including types you don't own, like `String` or `int` — without subclassing:

```dart
extension StringExtras on String {
  String get reversed => split('').reversed.join();
  bool get isPalindrome => this == reversed;
}

print('level'.isPalindrome); // true
print('dart'.reversed);       // trad
```

## Type aliases (`typedef`)

Beyond function types (see [Functions](/dart-lang/3.13.3/language/functions/)), `typedef` can alias any type for readability:

```dart
typedef UserId = String;
typedef UserMap = Map<UserId, String>;

UserMap users = {'u1': 'psdk'};
```

## `covariant`

When overriding a method in a subclass and narrowing a parameter type, mark it `covariant` to tell the analyzer this is intentional:

```dart
class Animal {
  void chase(Animal other) {}
}

class Cat extends Animal {
  @override
  void chase(covariant Cat other) {} // narrowed from Animal to Cat, intentionally
}
```

## Sealed classes and exhaustiveness (Dart 3+)

`sealed` restricts which classes can extend/implement a type to the same library — enabling the analyzer to check that a `switch` covers every case:

```dart
sealed class Shape {}

class Circle extends Shape {
  final double radius;
  Circle(this.radius);
}

class Square extends Shape {
  final double side;
  Square(this.side);
}

double area(Shape shape) => switch (shape) {
  Circle(radius: var r) => 3.14159 * r * r,
  Square(side: var s) => s * s,
  // no `default` needed — the analyzer knows these are the only two subtypes
};
```

If you later add a new subclass of `Shape` and forget to handle it in a `switch`, the analyzer will flag it as non-exhaustive.

Next: [Debugging](/dart-lang/3.13.3/language/debugging/).

Language.Files, Processes and IO:
# Files, Processes and I/O

`dart:io` provides everything for interacting with the file system, running other programs, environment variables, and low-level networking. It's available in standalone Dart (command-line/server apps) but **not** in Dart running in a browser.

```dart
import 'dart:io';
```

## Reading and writing files

```dart
import 'dart:io';

void main() async {
  final file = File('notes.txt');

  // Write (overwrites the file if it exists)
  await file.writeAsString('Hello from Dart\n');

  // Append
  await file.writeAsString('Another line\n', mode: FileMode.append);

  // Read the whole file as a String
  String contents = await file.readAsString();
  print(contents);

  // Read as lines
  List<String> lines = await file.readAsLines();
  print(lines);

  // Read raw bytes
  List<int> bytes = await file.readAsBytes();
}
```

Synchronous versions exist too (`writeAsStringSync`, `readAsStringSync`, etc.) — useful for small scripts where blocking briefly doesn't matter, but prefer the `async` versions in anything long-running like a server.

## Streaming large files

Reading a huge file into memory with `readAsString` isn't always ideal. Stream it instead:

```dart
final file = File('big.log');

await for (final line in file.openRead().transform(utf8.decoder).transform(LineSplitter())) {
  print(line);
}
```

`utf8` and `LineSplitter` come from `dart:convert` — see [JSON and Serialization](/dart-lang/3.13.3/language/json-and-serialization/) for more on that library.

## Checking existence, deleting, and file metadata

```dart
final file = File('notes.txt');

print(await file.exists());
print(await file.length());
FileStat stat = await file.stat();
print(stat.modified);

await file.delete();
```

## Working with directories

```dart
final dir = Directory('output');

if (!await dir.exists()) {
  await dir.create(recursive: true);
}

// List contents
await for (final entity in dir.list()) {
  print(entity.path);
}

// Current working directory
print(Directory.current.path);
```

## Environment variables

```dart
Map<String, String> env = Platform.environment;
print(env['HOME']);          // Linux: your home dir
print(env['USERPROFILE']);   // Windows: your home dir

print(Platform.isWindows);
print(Platform.isLinux);
print(Platform.numberOfProcessors);
```

## Running other processes

```dart
import 'dart:io';

void main() async {
  final result = await Process.run('ls', ['-la']);
  print(result.stdout);
  print(result.exitCode);

  if (result.exitCode != 0) {
    print('Error: ${result.stderr}');
  }
}
```

For a long-running process where you want to stream output as it happens rather than wait for it to finish:

```dart
final process = await Process.start('ping', ['-c', '4', 'example.com']);

process.stdout.transform(utf8.decoder).listen(print);
process.stderr.transform(utf8.decoder).listen(print);

final exitCode = await process.exitCode;
print('Exited with $exitCode');
```

Note the command differs by platform — `ping -c 4` (Linux) vs `ping -n 4` (Windows) — so cross-platform process code often needs `Platform.isWindows` branches.

## Standard input and output

```dart
import 'dart:io';

void main() {
  stdout.writeln('What is your name?');
  String? name = stdin.readLineSync();
  stdout.writeln('Hello, $name!');
}
```

`stdin.readLineSync()` blocks until the user presses Enter, returning `null` at end-of-input (e.g. piped input that's run out).

## Reading command-line input piped from another program

```bash
echo "psdk" | dart run bin/my_app.dart
```

```dart
void main() {
  String? line = stdin.readLineSync();
  print('Got: $line');
}
```

For structured flag/option parsing instead of raw `List<String> arguments`, see [Command-Line Argument Parsing](/dart-lang/3.13.3/guides/command-line-argument-parsing/) in Guides.

Next: [Isolates and Concurrency](/dart-lang/3.13.3/language/isolates-and-concurrency/).

Language.Isolates and Concurrency:
# Isolates and Concurrency

`async`/`await` (see [Async and Futures](/dart-lang/3.13.3/language/async-and-futures/)) lets Dart *interleave* work on a single thread — great for I/O-bound tasks like network calls, where you're mostly waiting. It does **not** give you true parallelism: a heavy CPU-bound computation (e.g. crunching a large list, parsing a huge file, image processing) will still block everything else, because it's all running on the same isolate.

**Isolates** are Dart's actual concurrency primitive: separate workers, each with their own memory and event loop, that communicate only by passing messages. There's no shared mutable state between isolates — this is intentional and avoids an entire class of concurrency bugs (race conditions, data races) that thread-based languages have to guard against manually.

## Why not just use threads?

Dart doesn't expose OS threads with shared memory directly. Instead, each isolate has its own heap. This means:

- No locks, mutexes, or shared-state race conditions to worry about.
- Communication happens explicitly via message-passing (`SendPort`/`ReceivePort`), so data crossing an isolate boundary is copied (or transferred, for some types), not shared.
- Spinning up an isolate has more overhead than starting an async task, so isolates are for genuinely parallel, often CPU-heavy work — not a replacement for everyday `async`/`await`.

## The simple case: `Isolate.run`

For a single, one-off computation, `Isolate.run` is the easiest entry point (Dart 2.19+):

```dart
import 'dart:isolate';

int heavyComputation(int n) {
  int total = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    total += i;
  }
  return total;
}

void main() async {
  print('Starting...');

  // Runs heavyComputation on a separate isolate, off the main thread
  int result = await Isolate.run(() => heavyComputation(100000000));

  print('Result: $result');
}
```

While `heavyComputation` runs, the main isolate's event loop is free — timers, other `await`s, and UI (in a Flutter context) keep responding.

## Manual isolates with `Isolate.spawn`

For longer-lived workers or two-way communication, spawn an isolate manually and talk to it via ports:

```dart
import 'dart:isolate';

void worker(SendPort mainSendPort) {
  final receivePort = ReceivePort();
  mainSendPort.send(receivePort.sendPort); // hand back a way to reply

  receivePort.listen((message) {
    if (message is int) {
      mainSendPort.send(message * 2);
    }
  });
}

void main() async {
  final mainReceivePort = ReceivePort();
  await Isolate.spawn(worker, mainReceivePort.sendPort);

  final events = StreamIterator(mainReceivePort);

  await events.moveNext();
  SendPort workerSendPort = events.current;

  workerSendPort.send(21);

  await events.moveNext();
  print('Doubled: ${events.current}'); // Doubled: 42
}
```

This is more boilerplate than `Isolate.run`, but necessary when you need to send multiple messages back and forth over the isolate's lifetime, rather than a single request/response.

## What can cross an isolate boundary

Only certain data can be sent through a `SendPort`: primitives (`int`, `double`, `String`, `bool`, `null`), `List`/`Map`/`Set` of those, `SendPort`s themselves, and a few special types like `TransferableTypedData`. You **cannot** send arbitrary objects, open file handles, or closures that capture non-sendable state — attempting to will throw at runtime.

```dart
// Fine to send:
mainSendPort.send({'name': 'psdk', 'scores': [1, 2, 3]});

// Not sendable — will throw:
// mainSendPort.send(File('data.txt'));
```

## When to reach for isolates

| Situation | Use |
| --- | --- |
| Waiting on a network request, file read, or database query | `async`/`await` — you're waiting, not computing. |
| Parsing a very large JSON payload, image processing, heavy math | An isolate — this is CPU work that would otherwise freeze everything else. |
| Many small, quick async operations | `async`/`await`, possibly with `Future.wait` for concurrency — spawning an isolate per task is overkill. |

Next: [JSON and Serialization](/dart-lang/3.13.3/language/json-and-serialization/).

Language.JSON and Serialization:
# JSON and Serialization

`dart:convert` provides JSON encoding/decoding out of the box — no external package required for basic use.

```dart
import 'dart:convert';
```

## Decoding JSON

```dart
String jsonString = '{"name": "psdk", "age": 30, "tags": ["dart", "linux"]}';

Map<String, dynamic> data = jsonDecode(jsonString);

print(data['name']); // psdk
print(data['age']);  // 30
print(data['tags']); // [dart, linux]
```

`jsonDecode` returns `dynamic` — for an object it's a `Map<String, dynamic>`, for an array it's a `List<dynamic>`. You're responsible for casting to the types you expect.

## Encoding to JSON

```dart
Map<String, dynamic> user = {
  'name': 'psdk',
  'age': 30,
  'tags': ['dart', 'linux'],
};

String jsonString = jsonEncode(user);
print(jsonString);
// {"name":"psdk","age":30,"tags":["dart","linux"]}
```

Pretty-printed output:

```dart
var encoder = JsonEncoder.withIndent('  ');
print(encoder.convert(user));
```

## Mapping JSON to your own classes (manual approach)

The common pattern is a class with a `fromJson` factory constructor and a `toJson` method:

```dart
class User {
  final String name;
  final int age;
  final List<String> tags;

  User({required this.name, required this.age, required this.tags});

  factory User.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json) {
    return User(
      name: json['name'] as String,
      age: json['age'] as int,
      tags: (json['tags'] as List).cast<String>(),
    );
  }

  Map<String, dynamic> toJson() {
    return {
      'name': name,
      'age': age,
      'tags': tags,
    };
  }
}

void main() {
  final json = jsonDecode('{"name":"psdk","age":30,"tags":["dart","linux"]}');
  final user = User.fromJson(json);
  print(user.name); // psdk

  print(jsonEncode(user.toJson()));
}
```

This is entirely hand-written — no reflection, no annotations, nothing generated. It's verbose for large models, but has zero build-time dependency and is easy to reason about.

## Handling missing or nullable fields safely

Real-world JSON is rarely as clean as your model. Guard against missing keys and wrong types:

```dart
factory User.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json) {
  return User(
    name: json['name'] as String? ?? 'Unknown',
    age: (json['age'] as num?)?.toInt() ?? 0,
    tags: json['tags'] is List
        ? (json['tags'] as List).cast<String>()
        : <String>[],
  );
}
```

## Nested objects and lists of objects

```dart
class Address {
  final String city;
  Address({required this.city});

  factory Address.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json) =>
      Address(city: json['city'] as String);

  Map<String, dynamic> toJson() => {'city': city};
}

class Company {
  final String name;
  final Address address;
  final List<String> employees;

  Company({required this.name, required this.address, required this.employees});

  factory Company.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json) {
    return Company(
      name: json['name'] as String,
      address: Address.fromJson(json['address'] as Map<String, dynamic>),
      employees: (json['employees'] as List).cast<String>(),
    );
  }

  Map<String, dynamic> toJson() => {
        'name': name,
        'address': address.toJson(),
        'employees': employees,
      };
}
```

## Code generation as an alternative (mentioned, not required)

For large projects with many models, hand-writing `fromJson`/`toJson` for every class gets repetitive. Packages like `json_serializable` (paired with `build_runner`) generate this boilerplate from annotations instead. That's a package-management and build-tooling topic — see [Working with Packages](/dart-lang/3.13.3/guides/working-with-packages/) for how to add packages, and the package's own documentation on pub.dev for its specific annotations and generated code, which are outside the scope of this guide.

## Encoding non-JSON-native types

`jsonEncode` only knows how to serialize `num`, `String`, `bool`, `null`, `List`, and `Map` directly. Anything else needs a `toJson()` — pass a `toEncodable` callback for objects that don't already have one:

```dart
class Point {
  final double x, y;
  Point(this.x, this.y);
}

String json = jsonEncode(
  Point(1, 2),
  toEncodable: (obj) {
    if (obj is Point) return {'x': obj.x, 'y': obj.y};
    return obj;
  },
);
```

Next: [Strings, Numbers and RegExp](/dart-lang/3.13.3/language/strings,-numbers-and-regexp/).

Language.Strings, Numbers and RegExp:
# Strings, Numbers and RegExp

Practical formatting, parsing, and pattern-matching operations that come up constantly in real code but don't fit neatly into the earlier [Variables and Types](/dart-lang/3.13.3/language/variables-and-types/) page.

## Parsing strings into numbers

```dart
int a = int.parse('42');
double b = double.parse('3.14');

// Safe versions that return null instead of throwing on bad input
int? c = int.tryParse('not a number'); // null
double? d = double.tryParse('3.14');    // 3.14

// num.parse picks int or double automatically
num e = num.parse('42');    // 42 (int)
num f = num.parse('3.14');  // 3.14 (double)
```

Always prefer `tryParse` over `parse` when the input isn't guaranteed valid (e.g. user input, external data) — `parse` throws a `FormatException` on failure, `tryParse` just gives you `null` to handle.

```dart
String? input = '12a';
int? value = int.tryParse(input);

if (value == null) {
  print('Invalid number: $input');
} else {
  print('Parsed: $value');
}
```

## Formatting numbers as strings

```dart
double price = 19.9;

print(price.toStringAsFixed(2));   // "19.90"
print(price.toStringAsPrecision(3)); // "19.9"
print(1234567.toString());          // "1234567"

int hex = 255;
print(hex.toRadixString(16));       // "ff"
print(int.parse('ff', radix: 16));  // 255

print(3.14159.round());  // 3
print(3.14159.floor());  // 3
print(3.14159.ceil());   // 4
print((-5).abs());        // 5
```

## Common `String` operations

```dart
String s = '  Hello, Dart!  ';

print(s.trim());            // "Hello, Dart!"
print(s.trim().toLowerCase()); // "hello, dart!"
print(s.trim().toUpperCase()); // "HELLO, DART!"

print('hello'.contains('ell'));   // true
print('hello'.startsWith('he'));  // true
print('hello'.endsWith('lo'));    // true
print('hello'.indexOf('l'));       // 2
print('hello'.replaceAll('l', 'L')); // heLLo
print('a,b,c'.split(','));          // [a, b, c]
print(['a', 'b', 'c'].join('-'));   // a-b-c

print('hi'.padLeft(5));       // "   hi"
print('hi'.padLeft(5, '0'));  // "000hi"
print('hi'.padRight(5, '*')); // "hi***"

print('hello'.substring(1, 4)); // "ell"
print('hello'[0]);               // "h" (index access)
print('hello'.length);           // 5
```

## Checking and comparing strings

```dart
print('' == ''); // true — value equality, not reference
print('abc'.compareTo('abd')); // negative — 'abc' sorts before 'abd'
print('Hello'.toLowerCase() == 'hello'); // true, after normalizing case

print(''.isEmpty);       // true
print('  '.trim().isEmpty); // true
print('x'.isNotEmpty);    // true
```

## `StringBuffer` for building strings in a loop

Repeated `+=` on a `String` creates a new string each time — fine for a handful of operations, wasteful in a loop. Use `StringBuffer` instead:

```dart
final buffer = StringBuffer();

for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  buffer.write('Line $i\n');
}

String result = buffer.toString();
print(result);
```

## Regular expressions

```dart
RegExp digitsOnly = RegExp(r'^\d+$');

print(digitsOnly.hasMatch('12345')); // true
print(digitsOnly.hasMatch('123a5')); // false

// Extracting matches
RegExp emailPattern = RegExp(r'[\w.+-]+@[\w-]+\.[\w.-]+');
String text = 'Contact: psdk@example.com or admin@example.org';

for (final match in emailPattern.allMatches(text)) {
  print(match.group(0));
}
// psdk@example.com
// admin@example.org
```

Capture groups:

```dart
RegExp datePattern = RegExp(r'(\d{4})-(\d{2})-(\d{2})');
final match = datePattern.firstMatch('Date: 2026-09-17');

if (match != null) {
  print('Year: ${match.group(1)}');  // 2026
  print('Month: ${match.group(2)}'); // 09
  print('Day: ${match.group(3)}');   // 17
}
```

Replacing with a regex:

```dart
String result = 'hello world'.replaceAll(RegExp(r'o'), '0');
print(result); // hell0 w0rld

String cleaned = '  extra   spaces   here  '.replaceAll(RegExp(r'\s+'), ' ').trim();
print(cleaned); // "extra spaces here"
```

The `r'...'` raw string prefix (see [Variables and Types](/dart-lang/3.13.3/language/variables-and-types/)) is almost always used for regex patterns, so backslashes (`\d`, `\s`, `\w`) don't need to be double-escaped.

